  "raw_text": "{\"options\": [\"The conclusion of the play presents Macbeth as having lost all sense of reality, as he states that life is merely a story fabricated by an idiot, reflecting his profound emptiness.\", \"By the end of the narrative, Macbeth declares that existence is a fabrication told by an idiot, signaling his complete spiritual and existential desolation.\", \"The final lines of the play reveal that Macbeth believes life has no substance, being merely a tale spun by an idiot, which underscores his total sense of alienation and void.\", \"Macbeth's final assertion that life is a tale told by an idiot serves as a testament to his loss of meaning and his deep inner emptiness at the play's conclusion.\", \"In the play's closing moments, Macbeth asserts that life is a story constructed by an idiot, illustrating his complete detachment from reality and his deep sense of void.\"]}\n",
